# rules: ruby-missing-auth
# languages: ruby
# fixture: ruby/CWE-306.rb
# from: CWE-306 Missing Authentication for Critical Function
#
# Ruby's half of `<language>-missing-auth` is the explicit skip rather than the
# absent guard, and that is a limit of what the syntax says. A Rails controller
# authenticates in `ApplicationController`, in a Rack middleware or in the
# routes, and a rule that reported every action in a controller which does not
# declare a filter itself would report every action in every Rails application
# there is. `skip_before_action :authenticate_user!` is the opposite: somebody
# wrote down that this action is open, and that is a statement a rule can read.
#
# A rule for the absent guard was written and is not here. Being file-level -
# "no authentication filter anywhere in this file" - it has no line in its own
# fixture that must stay quiet, and a fixture with no `ok:` cannot show a rule
# is not simply firing everywhere.
#
# Skipping authentication with skip_before_action :authenticate_user! or
# :require_login allows unauthenticated access. The rule flags any use of
# skip_before_action that disables an auth check; safe code keeps the
# before_action.

. as $root
| ( ($root | scan_ast(["*.rb"]; [
      "skip_before_action :authenticate_user!",
      "skip_before_action :authenticate_user!, $$$_",
      "skip_before_filter :authenticate_user!",
      "skip_before_filter :authenticate_user!, $$$_",
      "skip_before_action :require_login",
      "skip_before_action :require_login, $$$_"
    ])) as $all
    | ( $all | of(["skip_before_action :authenticate_user!","skip_before_action :authenticate_user!, $$$_","skip_before_filter :authenticate_user!","skip_before_filter :authenticate_user!, $$$_","skip_before_action :require_login","skip_before_action :require_login, $$$_"])
        | finding("ruby-missing-auth";
            "Authentication skipped via skip_before_action; unauthenticated users can reach this action") ) )
| report
